              "text": "While there are **four primary competitive strokes** recognized worldwide, there are about **eight major swimming styles** that are commonly taught for recreation, utility, and safety. \n\nIf you count historical, military, and novelty techniques, there are **well over a dozen** different swimming styles you can learn. \n\nA breakdown of the different swimming styles you can master is categorized by their use:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The 4 Competitive Strokes (The \"Big Four\")\nThese are the standard strokes regulated by World Aquatics and featured in the Olympics. They are excellent for cardiovascular fitness, speed, and endurance.\n*   **Freestyle (Front Crawl):** The fastest and most efficient swimming style. It involves alternating arm pull-throughs, a continuous flutter kick, and side-breathing.\n*   **Backstroke:** Essentially the front crawl flipped onto your back. It uses an alternating arm rotation and flutter kick. Because your face remains out of the water, it is popular for people who dislike putting their face in the water, though you cannot see where you are going.\n*   **Breaststroke:** The oldest and most popular recreational stroke. It uses a simultaneous, circular arm pull and a \"frog kick\" (whip kick). It is slower and highly technical but very gentle on the body.\n*   **Butterfly:** The most physically demanding and modern competitive stroke. It involves a symmetrical, overhead arm sweep combined with a continuous \"dolphin kick\" (where the legs move together like a mermaid's tail).\n\n---\n\n### 2. The 4 Safety, Utility, and Military Strokes\nThese strokes are generally learned for survival, lifesaving, military training, or long-distance endurance.\n*   **Elementary Backstroke:** A highly relaxing stroke swum on your back. It uses a breaststroke kick and synchronized arm movements entirely underwater. It is often taught to children using the mnemonic \"Chicken, Starfish, Soldier\" (representing the arm positions). It is used primarily for resting in the water.\n*   **Sidestroke:** Swum on your side using an asymmetric \"scissor kick\" and one-arm-at-a-time pulls. Because it keeps one arm completely free, it is the primary stroke used by lifeguards to tow a victim to safety.\n*   **Combat Sidestroke (CSS):** A variation of the sidestroke developed specifically for the U.S. Navy SEALs. It is a highly efficient, low-profile stroke that allows swimmers to move quickly and quietly through the water while wearing heavy gear and remaining nearly invisible from the surface.\n*   **Trudgen (or Trudgen Crawl):** Named after swimmer John Trudgen, this historical hybrid style combines the alternating overhand arm movements of the freestyle with the scissor kick of the sidestroke. It was highly popular in the late 19th century before the modern front crawl was fully developed.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Specialty and Novelty Styles\nIf you want to expand your skills even further, there are several informal or niche styles:\n*   **Dog Paddle:** The most basic, intuitive swimming motion. Swum with the head above water, using quick, alternating, paw-like arm pulls and a messy flutter kick. It is usually the very first instinctual style children or beginners use.\n*   **Survival Float / Survival Backstroke:** A low-energy technique designed to keep you afloat for hours in an emergency. It involves lying on your back and using minimal, gentle movements of the hands and feet to stay above water without exhausting yourself.\n*   **Corkscrew Swimming:** A fun drill where you continuously rotate your body, transitioning from one freestyle stroke to one backstroke stroke, twisting through the water like a corkscrew.\n*   **Monofin / Mermaid Swimming:** A style where both feet are slipped into a single large fin (a monofin). Swimmers use their entire core to execute dolphin kicks, mimicking the movements of marine mammals (or mythical mermaids).\n\n### Which ones should you learn?\nIf you are learning to swim, a great progression is:\n1.  **Dog Paddle & Survival Float** (for basic comfort and safety).\n2.  **Freestyle & Elementary Backstroke** (to build stamina, speed, and a reliable resting stroke).\n3.  **Breaststroke & Backstroke** (for versatility and joint-friendly exercise).\n4.  **Sidestroke & Butterfly** (for advanced fitness, coordination, and lifesaving skills).",
